     0.010          3.324        0.076              8.62
     0.020          3.188        0.068              7.26
     0.030          3.085        0.063              6.20
     0.050          2.937        0.056              4.76
     0.080          2.804        0.051              3.46
     0.120          2.705        0.047              2.46

  d log[a1(2500)/a1(500)] / d log f_star = -0.0754
  to drive the measured 3.19 to 1 would need f_star wrong by 6.67 dex, a factor 4726967 -- which would put the stars above the total mass.
  [PASS] 4a  the test is nearly free of the mass-to-light wall that blocks every galactic candidate in this hunt: the stellar fraction would have to be wrong by more than 1 dex to remove the effect
         (lever -0.0754; f_star would have to move 6.67 dex)

======================================================================================================================
VERDICT -- K2a (no single second acceleration in clusters)
======================================================================================================================
  1. THE MEASUREMENT REPRODUCES.  a1(2500)/a1(500) = 3.188, [2.83, 3.59], 0.068 dex, 20/20
     the same sign, on both footings, with a mutation control that returns 1.000000 exactly.
  2. THE FORMULA IS A RESTATEMENT and this script proves it in three lines plus a 1e-12 numerical closure.
     Only the two-point DISAGREEMENT is a measurement.
  3. AND THE DISAGREEMENT IS NOT DIAGNOSTIC.  A concordance NFW halo -- no second scale anywhere in it --
     run through the identical estimator gives a1 ratios 2.42 - 3.92 over c500 = 2-5, which
     BRACKETS the measured 3.19.  The estimator is measuring how NFW-like the mass profile is, which is
     the known cluster problem (Sanders 1999; The & White 1988; Angus+2008) wearing a new estimator.
  4. UPSILON LEVER: d log(ratio)/d log f_star = -0.0754; f_star would have to be wrong by 6.67 dex.
     This part of the candidate's claim stands and is worth keeping: unlike every galactic candidate in
     this hunt, the cluster two-point test is not blocked by the stellar mass-to-light ratio.
  CATEGORY: NOT Kepler-grade.  It is a correct negative result, its formula is a restatement, and its
  content is a known fact restated.  Criterion (4) fails: this has been stated before, in other clothes.

RESULT: 5 checks, 1 FAIL -> ["3a  THE DECISIVE CONTROL, AND IT CAN FAIL: does a concordance NFW halo -- an object with dark matter and no second acceleration scale of any kind -- reproduce the measured two-point ratio?  If it does, the 'kill' is a restatement of the known cluster problem in a new estimator"]
